----------------

For AI to have a good overview:

Key findings in existing code

- SolverDBM already implements a Laplace field solver and a discrete DBM-style arc/growth model (field, arc, boundary) — good starting point.
- Code uses floats, a Laplace-only approach, and a heuristic pick/weight growth (DBM). That models pattern growth but omits space charge, ionization kinetics, transport, and photoionization needed for realistic lightning/streamer physics.
- There’s existing infrastructure for file caching and OpenMP for parallelism.
Physics-focused recommendation summary (concise)

- Use SI units and double precision for fields, densities, and times.
- Replace Laplace-only solve with Poisson solver to include space charge (solve ∇·(ε∇φ) = −ρ).
- Add charge carriers and transport:
    - Electron and ion densities (ne, ni) on the grid.
    - Drift-diffusion equations (∂n/∂t = −∇·(μ n E) + ∇·(D ∇n) + source − loss).
    - Mobility μ(E, p, T), diffusion D.
- Add ionization chemistry:
    - Townsend ionization coefficient α(E,p), attachment, recombination rates.
    - Photoionization (Zheleznyak or simplified Helmholtz model) for air to allow streamer propagation in gaps.
- Seed stochastic avalanches for streamer branching and use stochastic source terms or particle-in-cell (PIC) for electrons for high fidelity.
- Use adaptive timestep and substepping (CFL and ionization-limited) and possibly AMR or local refinement near streamers.
- Boundary/electrode models: electron emission, secondary emission, charged surfaces, and Paschen-corrected breakdown thresholds.

Concrete near-term plan (recommended first implementation)

- Add a physics/Constants.hpp and physics/Parameters.hpp with SI constants and physical parameters.
- Refactor SolverDBM into Physics::DBM + Electrostatics modules; keep arc as a high-level visualization/indicator only.
- Implement Poisson solver (multigrid or conjugate-gradient) producing field E = −∇φ.
- Implement a 2D drift-diffusion time step for electrons with Townsend ionization source term (explicit/semi-implicit) and test Townsend avalanche in uniform field.
- Add a simple photoionization model and run streamer propagation tests.